  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Let $I=N$ and ${X}_{i}={-i,\\,0,\\,i},$ then ${\u22c3}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}=$",
  "worked_solution": "The union of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n{\u22c3}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one\u00a0}{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=1,$ we have ${X}_{1}={-1,0,1}$\n\n- if $i=2,$ we have ${X}_{2}={-2,0,2}$\n\n- if $i=3,$ we have ${X}_{3}={-3,0,3}$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that all the integers belong to one of the sets.\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u22c3}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}=Z.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
